#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""git ai-usage: record local AI coding-agent usage per commit in git notes.

Reads the Claude Code transcripts (~/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl) and the Codex rollout files
(~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl) on this machine and writes, for each of your commits, the
tokens and the derived cost of the work done in the repository since your previous commit. The
data goes to the notes ref refs/notes/ai-usage, so commit SHAs do not change and past commits can
be backfilled.

A commit gets the usage between your previous commit and its author date, from transcripts
whose working directory is inside the repository (or one of its worktrees). Each usage record
belongs to exactly one window, so no record is counted twice.

Note format, one line per tool and model (plus one line per tool for edited lines):

    claude-code model=claude-opus-4-1 in=1200 out=5400 cache_read=230000 cache_write=12000 cost_usd=1.8400 author=dev@example.com from=1791000000 to=1791003600
    claude-code added=120 deleted=14 author=dev@example.com from=1791000000 to=1791003600
    codex model=gpt-5.6-sol in=3100 out=900 cache_read=410000 cache_write=0 cost_usd=0.1940 author=dev@example.com from=1791000000 to=1791003600

Readers sum the numeric keys of the unique lines. A line is identified by its tool, its model (or
none, for edited lines) and its window (from, to), so a note copied twice by an amend is counted
once, a squash keeps the lines of both commits, and a line rewritten by `backfill --force` (new
prices, an author) replaces its older copy after a notes merge. `author` is the commit author
email, so a reader can attribute a note whose commit it cannot see (an unpushed or rewritten SHA).
"""

